1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,640
About two or three minutes ago, in answer, I replied to the chair.

2
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:05,220
You said, and I quote,

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:10,260
one of the things we'd like the Israelis to do is switch the water back on.

4
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:13,680
Now, that says that they turned it off.

5
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:17,540
It says that you recognise they have the power to turn it on.

6
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:23,680
Therefore, isn't turning water off and having the ability to turn it back on,

7
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:27,800
but choosing not to, isn't that a breach of international humanitarian law?

8
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:30,140
Well, it's just something they ought to do, in my opinion.

9
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:31,780
No, of course they should do it.

10
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:35,040
Every human being would say you don't cut people's water supply off,

11
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:38,400
but I'm asking you and your position as foreign secretary,

12
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:41,800
around the point of international humanitarian law,

13
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:46,940
if Israel have the power to turn the water back on that they turned off,

14
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:51,600
surely that is a flagrant breach of international humanitarian law.

15
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:53,820
Well, I'm not a lawyer.

16
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:56,160
My view is they ought to switch it on,

17
00:00:56,160 --> 00:01:00,660
because at the north of Gaza, the conflict is now effectively over there,

18
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:04,420
and so getting more water and power into northern Gaza

19
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:05,620
would be a very good thing to do.

20
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:07,740
You don't have to be a lawyer to make a judgement about that.

21
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:08,920
You just have to be a human being.

22
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:11,540
Forgive me, Sir Philip, under international obligations,

23
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:15,480
do occupying powers have an obligation to provide access to water, yes or no?

24
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,560
You're asking me a technical legal question.

25
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:20,300
Sir Philip, forgive me.

26
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:22,500
You and I have played this dance enough times.

27
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:24,920
We all know that under international law,

28
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:27,900
there is an obligation for occupying powers to provide water.

29
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:31,760
You're asking me a technical question about occupying powers

30
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:34,700
and what their obligations are in international law.

31
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:39,260
I imagine you're correct, Chair, but I'm also not a lie.

32
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:40,640
I also just would point out...

33
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:45,420
Philip, just bear in mind, we've come to such a good place working with you

34
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:49,340
because we have the confidence that you do know these details,

35
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:50,760
and that's what your colleagues say.

36
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:53,400
You know that it's not that you presume I'm correct.

37
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,460
That is the duty on an occupying power.

38
00:01:57,960 --> 00:02:00,660
Yeah, I think that is right.

39
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:06,680
So, yes, but I would also add that in answering your questions earlier

40
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:09,580
about occupying...

41
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:11,200
I'm not asking you to apply it to Israel.

42
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:13,720
The facts are, though, that they are required to.

